mcp-server-grafana for Debian
=============================

Packaged build of the upstream grafana/mcp-grafana server (Apache-2.0).
Fork: https://github.com/Vitexus/mcp-server-grafana (upstream base v2.3.0,
synced 2026-11-16).

Installation
------------

    sudo apt install mcp-server-grafana             # the server
    sudo apt install mcprack-mcp-server-grafana     # registers it in mcprack

The server runs over stdio: /usr/bin/mcp-server-grafana.

Configuration (environment)
---------------------------

Variable                        Secret  Meaning
GRAFANA_URL                     no      Grafana URL (default http://localhost:3000)
GRAFANA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN   yes     Service account token (recommended)
GRAFANA_USERNAME                no      Basic auth user (alternative to token)
GRAFANA_PASSWORD                yes     Basic auth password
GRAFANA_ORG_ID                  no      Organization ID
GRAFANA_EXTRA_HEADERS           no      JSON object with extra HTTP headers
GRAFANA_ALLOWED_URLS            no      Allowlist of Grafana URLs
GRAFANA_FORWARD_HEADERS         no      Request headers forwarded to Grafana
GRAFANA_READ_ONLY               no      true = start with --disable-write
GRAFANA_USAGE_STATS             no      disabled (Debian default) | enabled | log

Read-only mode
--------------

GRAFANA_READ_ONLY=true makes the launcher start the server with
--disable-write, so no create/update tools are registered. The mcprack
companion package sets it by default. Further upstream flags (for example
--disable-query or --enable-write-tools) can be passed as arguments.

Usage statistics
----------------

Upstream reports anonymous usage statistics to Grafana Labs by default. The
Debian launcher sets GRAFANA_USAGE_STATS=disabled unless you set it
yourself (GRAFANA_USAGE_STATS=enabled to opt in, =log to inspect the payload).

Build
-----

Upstream requires Go 1.26. On trixie it is built with golang-1.26-go from
trixie-backports (enabled in debian/Jenkinsfile); GOTOOLCHAIN=local prevents
any toolchain download. Go modules are fetched from the Go module proxy while
building.
